# spira.conf.example — copy to `spira.conf` and edit. THE ONE CONFIGURATION SURFACE.
#
# Copy it to whichever of these the harness should read; the first one that exists wins:
#
#     <the checkout the harness runs from>/spira.conf
#     ${XDG_CONFIG_HOME:-$HOME/.config}/spira/spira.conf
#     /etc/spira/spira.conf
#
# or point $SPIRA_CONF at it directly. Keeping it OUTSIDE the checkout is the safer default:
# these are your paths, and none of them belong in a repository you might push.
#
# TWO KINDS OF KEY IN THIS FILE
#
#   TRAP KEYS (marked !! below) — keys whose derived default is plausibly wrong for a fresh
#   clone. configure.sh asks about them; install.sh refuses when they are unset. Set these
#   before running install.sh. They are collected at the top of this file.
#
#   DERIVABLE KEYS — every other key. The code carries a sensible default for each, derived
#   from where the harness is installed, so a clean clone runs without setting any of them.
#
# configure.sh writes your trap-key answers as active lines and shows every derivable key as
# a comment with its computed value, so you can see the defaults and override only what you
# need to change.

# EVERY KEY BELOW IS OPTIONAL. The code carries a default for each, derived from where the
# harness is installed, so a clean clone runs. What it cannot derive is where your database
# is, where your binaries live and which repositories exist, which is what you are here for.
#
# FORMAT: `KEY = value`, one per line, `#` comments, blank lines ignored. Values are NOT
# shell — `~` and `$HOME` expand and nothing else does, because this file is read by the
# process that summons agents and `$(...)` in it would be a command run as you. An
# unrecognised key is reported on stderr and ignored rather than obeyed, so a typo is not a
# setting you believe is in force.
#
# THE ENVIRONMENT WINS over this file, which is what keeps the test suites isolated: a suite
# sets SPIRA_DB to a throwaway database and this file cannot point it back at yours.
#
# SPIRA_HOME and SPIRA_REPO are deliberately NOT settable here. Where the harness is, is not
# a configuration question — it is a fact about where conf.sh sits. Letting a file answer it
# breaks the landing gate, which extracts a branch to a scratch tree and runs that tree's own
# suites: the config would point them back at the installed copy, so the gate would test the
# code already in force instead of the code being judged, and pass.

# ---- TRAP KEYS !! — set these; the derived default is plausibly wrong ---------------
#
# Run `configure.sh` to be asked for each of these interactively, or set them by hand.
# The marker `!!` in section headings below also marks individual trap keys elsewhere.

# !! SPIRA_PROD — the production checkout's harness subdir.
# systemd executes every unit's ExecStart from this path. The derived default assumes a
# directory named "<harness-repo>-prod" beside the dev checkout — that directory does not
# exist on a fresh clone, and install.sh refuses to write units whose ExecStart target is
# absent. Set this to the actual path before running install.sh.
#
# What it derives to without this key:
#   ${SPIRA_WORKSPACES}/${SPIRA_HOME_REPO}-prod/<harness-subdir>
#   e.g.  /srv/checkouts/spira-harness-prod/spira
# SPIRA_PROD = /srv/checkouts/spira-harness-prod/spira

# !! SPIRA_MAX_AEONS — aeon pool ceiling (task workers, not counting lane fayths).
# Default is 4. This is a guess about the operator's core count; a single-core box should
# use 1 or 2; a many-core box can go higher. The fleet ceiling (SPIRA_MAX_LIVE_AEONS)
# is the right knob when an API account limit matters more than core count.
# SPIRA_MAX_AEONS = 4

# !! SPIRA_MAX_LIVE_AEONS — whole-fleet ceiling (pool + lane fayths combined).
# Empty means no ceiling — correct when the constraint is cores, wrong when the constraint
# is a single API account shared between all aeons and the operator's own sessions.
# Set it to the total sessions your account can run simultaneously.
# SPIRA_MAX_LIVE_AEONS =

# !! SPIRA_LOOM_ADDR — where Loom (the live-graph server) listens.
# Default is 127.0.0.1:8788 (loopback only). Loom has no authentication in front of it;
# changing this to 0.0.0.0 exposes your beads database to every host on the LAN.
# SPIRA_LOOM_ADDR = 127.0.0.1:8788

# !! SPIRA_DOLT_DATA — the Dolt server's own data directory.
# Empty (the default) means you manage the Dolt server yourself; install.sh will not
# install dolt-beads.service. Set it to the directory dolt sql-server reads from if
# you want this harness to start and supervise the server.
# SPIRA_DOLT_DATA = /srv/beads

# ---- the database ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# The beads database, as a directory `bd -C` is pointed at. Everything the harness raises,
# reads, answers and closes lives here, including the statute book.
#
# THE DEFAULT IS OUTSIDE ANY REPOSITORY, and keep yours that way. A beads database
# accumulates internal working notes and agent memories; a path inside a checkout is one
# `git add -A` away from publishing them (law-beads-is-never-public).
#
# Default: ${XDG_DATA_HOME:-$HOME/.local/share}/spira/db
# SPIRA_DB = $HOME/.local/share/spira/db

# ---- your repositories ------------------------------------------------------------------
# The `repo:` label a bead means when it carries none — your home repository, which must have
# a row in repo-map.
# Default: the basename of the checkout the harness is installed in.
# SPIRA_HOME_REPO = home

# The map from `repo:<name>` to a checkout. Copy `repo-map.example` to `repo-map` beside it,
# or beside this file, and write your own rows. Unset, the harness looks for `repo-map` next
# to this file, then next to conf.sh, then falls back to `repo-map.example`.
# SPIRA_REPO_MAP = $HOME/.config/spira/repo-map

# Where your checkouts live. Used for the disk-headroom vital sign watchtower reports and
# for classifying repositories during a migration — not for locating anything, which is
# repo-map's job.
# Default: the parent of the checkout the harness is installed in.
# SPIRA_WORKSPACES = /srv/checkouts

# The root epic every plan hangs from. The sentinel reports progress against it.
# SPIRA_GOAL = sp-spira

# The list of what should be watching: `name|kind|target[|health]` rows. One `daemon` row is
# one systemd unit, so this file is what `systemd/install.sh` enables and disables against —
# adding a watcher is a row plus an install run, never a new unit file. Point it outside the
# checkout if your rows name paths you would rather not push.
# Default: `watchers` beside the harness.
# SPIRA_WATCHERS = $HOME/.config/spira/watchers

# Which of a watcher's lines a reader is shown by default: an extended regular expression that
# `watchd.sh drain` and `watchd.sh tail` BOTH match against the whole line, so that the command
# a session hook advertises and the command a session latches with cannot disagree about what
# matters. Everything else stays in the log, where `--all` and the file itself still reach it.
#
# ACTIONABLE means something is stuck, something broke, something needs a human choice, or a
# thing being waited on finished. Set this if your watchers speak a different vocabulary; every
# line that matches becomes something a session reacts to, so a filter that matches everything
# is the same as no watcher at all.
#
# Empty is REFUSED rather than read as "no filter" — as a regular expression it would match
# every line, and `--all` is how you ask for that on purpose.
# Default: ANSWERED|COMMENTED|ESCALAT|STRANDED|POISON|DEGRADED|BLOCKED|UNREACHABLE|FAIL|ERROR|LANDED|⚠
# SPIRA_ACTIONABLE = ANSWERED|STRANDED|FAIL|ERROR

# The id prefix of your own beads, without the hyphen. `watchd.sh health-ids` looks for it to
# prove a watcher is still reading THIS database rather than one retired underneath it.
#
# It asks whether the state names ONE of your ids, and not whether it names a foreign one —
# a database here may legitimately hold beads imported under other prefixes, so "names
# something that is not ours" is true of a healthy watcher and would pass on a blind one.
# Default: the prefix of SPIRA_GOAL, because the goal is a bead in this database.
# SPIRA_ID_PREFIX = acme

# How long a health command in the watcher manifest may run, in seconds. A probe is your own
# command and `watchd.sh status` is what a session hook runs at every start, so an unbounded
# one hangs the opening of a context window rather than merely being slow. Running out of time
# is DEGRADED: it did not prove the watcher can see anything.
# Default: 10
# SPIRA_HEALTH_TIMEOUT = 30

# How long an actionable event may wait with no reader before it is escalated through a channel
# that needs no session, in seconds. `spira-watch-notify.timer` is what enforces it.
#
# The window has to be comfortably longer than an ordinary gap between a watcher emitting and
# somebody latching, because every escalation inside that gap is a false alarm — and it has to
# be far shorter than the hours a nobody-is-running interval actually lasts. Escalating is once
# per backlog rather than once per pass, so lengthening this delays the one ask you get; it does
# not make a standing condition quieter. If a class of line is never worth waking anyone for,
# narrow SPIRA_ACTIONABLE instead.
# Default: 1800
# SPIRA_NOTIFY_AGE = 900

# Where the answer watcher keeps what it has already seen. One key rather than two literals,
# because the watcher writes this file and the health assertion pointed at it reads the same
# path — the two disagreeing renders a permanent DEGRADED against a watcher that is working.
# Default: answered-seen.json under SPIRA_RUN.
# SPIRA_ANSWER_STATE = /var/lib/spira/answered-seen.json

# The two cursor files answered-since.sh writes — one per answer leg. Both default under
# SPIRA_RUN; override here only if you want them somewhere else.
# Default: answered-mark and answered-comment-mark under SPIRA_RUN.
# SPIRA_ANSWER_MARK = /var/lib/spira/answered-mark
# SPIRA_ANSWER_COMMENT_MARK = /var/lib/spira/answered-comment-mark

# Where the harness records which beads it closed itself, so its own closes are not
# announced back as operator verdicts by watch-answers.sh and answered-since.sh.
# Default: self-closed under SPIRA_RUN.
# SPIRA_SELF_CLOSED = /var/lib/spira/self-closed

# ---- the session hook ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# The coding agent's OWN settings file — the one the client reads, not one of ours. The session
# hook is registered in it by `spira/install-session-hook.sh`, and `spira/doctor.sh` reports
# whether it still is. Point this elsewhere if your client keeps its settings somewhere else.
# Default: $HOME/.claude/settings.json
# SPIRA_CLIENT_SETTINGS = $HOME/.config/some-agent/settings.json

# How many lines the session hook may spend, as a ceiling. Its output is prepended to a context
# window that has just opened, which is the most expensive place text can go — so the status
# table and the latch commands are printed whole and the preview of unread events is given
# exactly what is left. Raise it if you watch many things; 0 means no budget at all, and a
# large backlog will then print in full.
# Default: 32
# SPIRA_HOOK_LINES = 50

# ---- where your binaries are --------------------------------------------------------------
# Directories PREPENDED to PATH for the harness and everything it spawns. Everything here runs
# from systemd and from tmux, where a login shell's PATH does not exist, so `bd` in ~/.local/bin
# or a shim directory ahead of it has to be named. The tail (~/.local/bin, /usr/local/bin,
# /usr/bin, /bin) is always appended and is not configurable.
#
# If the harness tells you a program is missing and you know it is installed, this is the key.
# SPIRA_PATH = /opt/beads/bin

# ---- the cockpit --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The database the attention panel and the ops pane read. Defaults to SPIRA_DB, and there is
# very little reason to differ.
# COCKPIT_DB = $HOME/.local/share/spira/db

# How much of the LEFT COLUMN the attention panel takes, as a percentage of the window's
# height. The left column holds your session on top and the panel beneath it.
# COCKPIT_BOTTOM_PCT = 28

# How wide the ops dashboard column is, as a percentage of the window. It is a full-height
# column down the right-hand side, so this is the only dimension it has. A 13-inch laptop
# and a 32-inch monitor do not want the same width.
# COCKPIT_RIGHT_PCT = 33

# The directory the cockpit's panes open in. The top pane holds YOUR session, so this decides
# which project's instructions that session loads — not a cosmetic choice.
# Default: SPIRA_WIKI if you set one, otherwise the harness checkout.
# COCKPIT_CWD = $HOME/notes

# The attention panel binary. Build it with `cargo build --release` in cockpit/panel.
# Default: <cockpit>/panel/target/release/panel
# SPIRA_PANEL = $HOME/.local/bin/spira-panel

# The script an escalation is delivered through. Anything that takes the same arguments as
# `cockpit/ask.sh add` will do, which is the seam for delivering somewhere else entirely.
# Default: <cockpit>/ask.sh
# SPIRA_NOTIFY = $HOME/.local/bin/my-pager

# Your name, as it appears in prose the harness writes for you to read.
# SPIRA_OPERATOR = the operator

# The actor name YOUR OWN comments are recorded under, so the attention panel can tell a reply
# of yours from a reply of the agent's. Both write into the same bead thread, and a panel that
# cannot separate them announces the agent's own comment back to it as your answer.
# SPIRA_OPERATOR_ACTOR = operator

# The label that means "waiting on you". It is the one the escalation gate defers on, the one
# every persona's predicate excludes, and the one the attention panel reads — one key rather
# than five literals, because five literals is how those four come to disagree.
#
# CHANGING IT ON A LIVE INSTALLATION ORPHANS every bead already carrying the old value: they
# keep the old label, and nothing is looking for it any more. Relabel them, or leave it alone.
# SPIRA_ASK_LABEL = needs-operator

# The label that means "parked on a CI run". An aeon puts it on a bead once its pull request is
# open, so nothing pays for a session to sit and watch a test suite; the sweep takes it off when
# the run resolves. Same argument as the ask label for it being one key: the sweep, both
# personas, the stranded-work report and the ops pane must agree on it, and the pane is the half
# nobody notices is wrong, because it simply shows fewer.
# SPIRA_CI_LABEL = awaiting-ci

# How long a park may last, in seconds, before the sweep treats it as lost rather than parked.
#
# THE LABEL IS EXCLUDED FROM EVERY PREDICATE AND FROM THE STRANDED-WORK REPORT — that is what
# stops parked work looking abandoned, and it is also why a park nothing will ever end is worse
# than a stall: not claimable, not reported, and shown as "in CI", which is the one description
# that stops anybody looking for the real cause. Past the longest run your CI can plausibly
# take, the promise that something else is watching is false, so the sweep unparks the bead and
# lets the report that would have found it find it.
#
# Ninety minutes is longer than any run this was written against; raise it if yours are slower.
# 0 disables the deadline and reinstates the permanent invisible park — a deliberate choice, not
# a default. Parks in repositories whose land mode is not `pr` are stripped regardless: those
# open no pull request, so there is no run to wait for at any deadline.
# SPIRA_CI_PARK_MAX = 5400

# How long a gate verdict may be reused, in seconds.
#
# The gate computes each verdict once and keys it by everything the verdict depends on that it
# can name: the branch's tree, the base commit, the changed file list, the repository's own gate
# command and the harness itself. A second caller with the same key — an aeon's own gate and
# then the landing pass, minutes later, on the same tree — is asking a question that has already
# been answered, and reuses the answer instead of paying for the suites and for the queue in
# front of the gate's worktree.
#
# WHAT THE KEY CANNOT NAME IS THE BOX: the toolchain the command ran under, what was installed
# beside it, what the network answered. Those drift while the key stands still, which makes a
# verdict a claim about a moment as well as about a tree, and this is how long that moment
# lasts. A day spans a branch's whole life from an aeon's gate to its landing, which is the
# reuse worth having, and bounds the drift to something a re-run resolves.
#
# The landing pass deletes entries at this same age, so there is one answer to how long a
# verdict lives rather than a reader's and a janitor's. 0 disables reuse: every entry reads as
# expired and every gate runs its suites.
# SPIRA_VERDICT_TTL = 86400

# When a prior red verdict still applies (same key, within TTL), testenv-batch.sh refuses to
# re-run. Set this to a sentence of at least 10 characters explaining why the prior red was
# not caused by the code — e.g. "runner host was destroyed by hypervisor OOM" — and the
# refusal is lifted for that one invocation. A bare flag or short string is rejected.
# Recorded in the verdict file so later readers can weigh the override.
# SPIRA_VERDICT_REPEAT_CONSIDERED =

# ---- the suites, and where each of them runs -----------------------------------------------
# Which suites the LANDING GATE runs, as a file of repository-relative paths with the reason
# each earns the wait beside it. `spira/suites.sh` reads the same file and runs everything the
# `spira/test-*.sh` glob finds that it does NOT name — so the gated set and the timed set are
# complements by construction and no suite can fall between them.
#
# What suites EXIST is the glob and never a list. The list used to live inside the gate where
# nothing else could read it, so nothing could answer "which suites does the gate not run":
# nine suites, four gated, five executed by nothing at all. A suite nobody runs is not a cheap
# test, it is a false record of coverage, and worse than no suite.
#
# The gate itself reads the copy in the tree UNDER TRIAL rather than this key, for the same
# reason SPIRA_HOME is not settable: it extracts a branch to a scratch tree and must judge that
# tree, so a configured path would point it back at the code already in force.
# SPIRA_GATE_SUITES = <the harness>/gate-suites

# Where the timed run records what it found — one file per suite, holding a status, the epoch
# it was written and how long the suite took. A GREEN CYCLE HAS TO LEAVE A POSITIVE RECORD:
# without one, "no bead was filed" reads identically whether every suite passed or the runner
# has not run since the box came up, and an empty directory gives the reassuring reading.
# SPIRA_SUITES_STATE = <the runtime directory>/suites

# How long one timed pass may take, and how long any one suite inside it may run, in seconds.
# The pass runs INSIDE an Ops session whose own wall systemd enforces, so the budget is under
# it with room for the session to read the sweep and write up what it found. A pass that runs
# out of budget stops cleanly and leaves a cursor, so the suites it did not reach LEAD the next
# pass rather than being the ones that are never run. The per-suite timeout is one key for both
# the gate and the timed pass, because a suite affordable in one and not the other is a suite
# whose cost nobody has decided.
# SPIRA_SUITES_BUDGET = 420
# SPIRA_SUITE_TIMEOUT = 600

# The priority a red from the timed pass is filed at. Routine by default: that pass blocks
# nothing and reopens nothing, and a failure caught twenty minutes after landing is fine when a
# revert undoes it. A suite covering something where that is not true says so ITSELF, with a
# `# priority: N` line beside its `# covers:` line — how urgent a failure is, is a claim only
# the suite's author can make, and a central table of it would be a second list to keep in step
# with the glob.
# SPIRA_SUITES_PRIORITY = 2

# How old a suite's result may be before it stops being evidence, in seconds. Past this the
# sweep reports the suite as unrun rather than as green, because a stale pass and a runner that
# has stopped are the same silence from outside. Keep it longer than the interval at which the
# sweep names the scan, so an ordinary quiet hour does not read as a fault.
# SPIRA_SUITES_STALE = 21600

# ---- the read surface over the live graph --------------------------------------------------
# !! Where Loom listens: one route serving the live beads and the dependency edges between them,
# and the page that draws them. LOCALHOST BY DEFAULT, deliberately — a bead carries internal
# working notes and your own judgement, so which interfaces can reach it is a decision to make
# rather than one a default should make for you. `0.0.0.0:8788` puts it on the LAN.
# SPIRA_LOOM_ADDR = 127.0.0.1:8788

# The deadline on ONE `bd` query behind that endpoint, in milliseconds. A query per request is
# the simple choice and it is the right one only while it stays cheap; this is the number that
# says when it has stopped being. Past it the request is REFUSED rather than served late, and
# every query's wall time is reported in the response either way. The shipped value is a
# measured p95 plus the tail a contended box adds. Raise it on slower hardware — and treat
# having had to as the finding it is, rather than as a formality.
# SPIRA_LOOM_BUDGET_MS = 500

# How long a parsed snapshot is held, in seconds. This bounds the cost by TIME rather than by
# viewer, so ten open tabs and a page that re-fetches cost one query instead of ten. It is a
# cache and not a background job: nothing runs when nobody is looking. 0 disables it, which
# makes every request pay for its own query.
# SPIRA_LOOM_CACHE_S = 15

# The compiled Loom binary. The default is `loom/target/release/loom` inside the harness
# checkout; set this if you install it elsewhere or manage the build separately.
# Default: <harness checkout>/loom/target/release/loom
# SPIRA_LOOM_BIN = $HOME/.local/bin/loom

# THE SPIKE PARTITION. A spike is a persona that researches one question to a costed document
# and carries no conversation; these three keys are what the harness reads to find its beads,
# to tell it where to write, and to refuse to merge anything else it leaves behind.
#
# The label its beads carry, and the label its fayth's predicate is built from — one key
# rather than four literals, because four literals is how four programs come to disagree
# about which beads exist.
# SPIRA_SPIKE_LABEL = spike
#
# Where a spike writes its document, relative to the root of whatever repository its bead
# names. Point it at wherever your own notes live.
# SPIRA_SPIKE_DIR = docs/spikes
#
# The path prefixes a spike branch is allowed to LAND, space-separated, defaulting to
# SPIRA_SPIKE_DIR alone. A spike is closed by its document; a proof of concept is evidence FOR
# that document, so it belongs on a branch of its own, named in the document, and unmerged.
# The landing worker refuses a spike branch that changes anything outside these trees. Widen
# it if the sources a spike preserves live somewhere other than beside the document — for
# example, "notes/spikes sources".
# SPIRA_SPIKE_PATHS = docs/spikes

# THE SCOPE LABEL. Every persona predicate is built as "$SPIRA_SCOPE_LABEL,<partition>", so
# this one key is the runtime dial that decides which beads the fleet can see.
#
# Default: the home repo's name (SPIRA_HOME_REPO). An install in a repo named "myproject"
# gets "myproject" automatically; an install in "spira" is unchanged.
#
# Two values that also matter:
#   myproject — beads must carry this label to be claimable by this install's fleet
#   (empty)   — no scope restriction; the partition label alone (e.g. "plan") is sufficient
#
# When empty, any bead carrying the right partition label (plan, incident, spike, ...) is
# visible. Set this only when you want to override the default derivation.
# SPIRA_SCOPE_LABEL = myproject

# CZAR STAGE MODE — per-class shadow/act for the queue sovereign.
# Each class of queue-state escalation (deadlock, attribution-failed, sort-failed,
# loop-stalled, ci-stalled, starved, ci-red) has its own stage key. The default is
# shadow: the czar investigates and writes CZAR-WOULD notes but changes nothing in the
# queue. Set a class to act once you have watched one shadow run and trust the verdict.
#
# shadow (default) — czar writes CZAR-WOULD notes and changes nothing in the queue
# act              — czar acts as it normally would; mutations are permitted
#
# SPIRA_CZAR_STAGE_DEADLOCK = shadow
# SPIRA_CZAR_STAGE_ATTRIBUTION_FAILED = shadow
# SPIRA_CZAR_STAGE_SORT_FAILED = shadow
# SPIRA_CZAR_STAGE_LOOP_STALLED = shadow
# SPIRA_CZAR_STAGE_CI_STALLED = shadow
# SPIRA_CZAR_STAGE_STARVED = shadow
# SPIRA_CZAR_STAGE_CI_RED = shadow

# The timezone dates are written in. Empty means the host's own, which is right until the host
# is a server in one zone and you read its output in another — the case where an evening's
# work lands under tomorrow's date and a chronological log stops being chronological.
# SPIRA_TZ = America/Los_Angeles

# ---- optional, and EMPTY BY DEFAULT --------------------------------------------------------
# Each of these names something you may simply not have. Every caller treats empty as "skip
# this", never as "guess": an optional call is not a dependency, a hard path is.

# A predecessor harness being retired, if you are migrating from one. Empty means there is
# none, and the tools that report on it say so and stop rather than printing a page of zeroes
# that reads like a clean drain.
# SPIRA_TOWN =

# A design document the judgement tier is given as context when it is asked why the graph is
# stalled.
# SPIRA_DESIGN = $HOME/notes/spira.md

# A JSONL mirror of a predecessor's databases, and the exporter that writes one. Used only by
# the migration importer.
# SPIRA_MIRROR =
# SPIRA_EXPORTER =

# Your wiki or notes checkout, if you keep one. The harness never requires it: where it wants
# to publish something there — the boundary table, the runbook page — it writes if this is set
# and carries on in silence if it is not. An optional call is not a dependency; a hard path is.
# SPIRA_WIKI = $HOME/notes

# A hook called where the harness wants an effect on that repository — regenerating whatever
# it renders. Absent, the harness carries on.
# SPIRA_WIKI_HOOK = $HOME/notes/bin/synth-statutes

# THE VIEW FOLLOWER: your program for keeping the attention surface pointed at whatever you
# should be looking at right now — the review when a review is live, the session otherwise.
# The surface is yours, so the harness ships no such program; it ships the row that watches
# one. Empty means you have none, and the manifest's `?view` row renders as `off` instead of
# being started.
#
# THE CONTRACT IT MUST MEET, so the manifest can both run it and judge it:
#
#   <prog> watch     a loop that enacts the state. This is what the unit starts.
#   <prog> status    prints a line `want: <session>` naming the multiplexer session that
#                    SHOULD be visible.
#
# That one line is the whole of the health assertion. A follower flips the view on
# TRANSITIONS, so one that has died misses them in silence — a stopped follower and a healthy
# idle one look identical in a process listing, a unit state and an unread count alike. Asking
# it what should be visible and the multiplexer what IS makes the difference measurable.
# SPIRA_VIEW = $HOME/.local/bin/view-follower

# The multiplexer session whose visible window the follower steers. Consulted only when
# SPIRA_VIEW names something.
# SPIRA_VIEW_SESSION = cockpit

# The alert units whose failure should be filed as an incident bead, as a find(1) name
# pattern — `*` `?` and `[...]` work, brace expansion does not. Empty means none, and
# install-intake.sh then says so rather than wiring whatever happens to match.
#
# NAME ONLY UNITS WHOSE FAILURE IS ACTIONABLE. Where alert templates are rendered per
# environment, most usually belong to development instances that fail routinely and by design,
# and wiring those fills the queue with events that have no action.
# SPIRA_ALERT_GLOB = alert-prod@.service

# !! The Dolt server's own data directory — NOT the beads project directory above. `bd -C` is
# pointed at the project; this is where the server keeps every database it serves. Set it only
# if you want this harness to supervise the server with a systemd unit; empty means you run
# the server yourself and `systemd/install.sh` will not install dolt-beads.service.
# SPIRA_DOLT_DATA = /srv/beads

# ---- host policy ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Which personas this host summons, space-separated. Unset means every fayth in the chamber.
# Deliberately NOT exported into children: it is policy for this host, and exporting it once
# reached a test suite through systemd and made the suite assert this host's roster instead of
# the defaults it was written to check (law-gates-run-in-a-clean-environment).
# SPIRA_FAYTHS = builder ops

# !! How many aeons may run at once. The admission throttle (sentinel CHECK 7) can hold new
# summons at zero when the queue is deep and draining, but never lowers this ceiling itself.
# Default 4. See TRAP KEYS section above.
# SPIRA_MAX_AEONS = 4

# !! The whole-fleet ceiling (pool + lane fayths). Empty means no ceiling. See TRAP KEYS above.
# SPIRA_MAX_LIVE_AEONS =

# ---- what the account spends ----------------------------------------------------------------
# The rolling window the plan is billed against, in hours. Every token figure on the dashboard
# is "inside the window", so this decides what those numbers mean. It is a fact about the plan
# rather than about the box, and a colleague on a different plan reads a different length.
# SPIRA_TOKEN_WINDOW_H = 5

# Where the client writes interactive session transcripts. The aeons' own traces live under the
# runtime directory and need no key, because the harness put them there. Point this at the wrong
# place and the session half of the split reads zero — which is the reading that looks like good
# news, so it is a key rather than a guess.
# SPIRA_TOKEN_PROJECTS = $HOME/.claude/projects

# Where the session transcripts are KEPT, which is not where the client writes them: that
# directory is unversioned, sits on whatever volume the home directory does, and promises
# nothing about retention. The transcripts are the only record of every decision taken in
# conversation that never became a bead, so this is a copy that outlives both. It defaults
# under the runtime directory because that is gitignored — the bodies carry paths,
# credentials read aloud and everything anyone ever said, and a default inside a shared
# checkout is one `git add -A` away from publishing all of it. Point it at whichever volume
# has the room; nothing here ever deletes, because retention is your decision to make.
# SPIRA_ARCHIVE = $HOME/.local/share/spira/archive

# The thresholds a LIVE session's carried context is measured against, shared by the status line
# and the dashboard so the two cannot disagree about how close to the edge a session is. Context
# is re-read in full on every turn, so these are the numbers that decide whether to clear.
# SPIRA_CTX_WARN = 200000
# SPIRA_CTX_HIGH = 400000
# SPIRA_CTX_LIMIT = 1000000
